 hematologic malignancy given blast morphology", "Autoimmune hemolytic anemia: serum studies show no metabolic derangements to support this"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional symptoms (weight loss, night sweats)", "Uncharacteristically low visual acuity (20/100 in both eyes)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.062, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The lack of documented myeloid markers weakens support for myeloid neoplasm, though this is a weak argument given the high blast count and pancytopenia."]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.190562613430127, "posterior": 0.5831736044398012, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Constitutional symptoms and blast percentage (35%) provide moderate support for lymphoid neoplasm, though blast counts this high are more typical of myeloid ra…"], "evidence_against": ["Normal CD4+/CD56+ status and lack of skin/endocrine findings slightly reduce lymphoid neoplasm likelihood, as these markers could differentiate lymphoid from o…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "lymphoblastic lymphoma"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.338, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["A 35% blast count exceeds typical thresholds for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms in chronic/accelerated phases (usually <20%), providing moderate evidence…"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.017, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Normal calcium and BUN levels reduce the likelihood of plasma cell disorders, which frequently present with hypercalcemia and renal impairment."]}, "kb_reactive_non_malignant_l": {"id": "kb_reactive_non_malignant_l", "label": "reactive / non-malignant leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.0, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The subacute presentation with significant blast percentage (35%) and constitutional symptoms provides moderate support for myeloid neoplasm over non-malignant…"]}}, "frontier": ["B2"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute presentation with constitutional symptoms and pancytopenia support chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm in accelerated phase?", "summary": "The subacute course with constitutional symptoms and pancytopenia is consistent with accelerated phase chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm, though the high blast count (35%) reduces this likelihood."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of blasts in peripheral blood support plasma cell disorder over other hematologic malignancies?", "summary": "Blasts in peripheral blood are inconsistent with plasma cell disorders, which typically show plasma cell infiltration rather than blasts in CBC. This moderately argues against plasma cell disorder."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of specific myeloid markers (e.g., CD4+/CD56+) in the case text inconsistent with a myeloid neoplasm diagnosis?", "summary": "The lack of documented myeloid markers weakens support for myeloid neoplasm, though this is a weak argument given the high blast count and pancytopenia."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the absence of skin/endocrine abnormalities and normal CD4+/CD56+ status in PrimeKG discriminators inconsistent with lymphoid neoplasm?", "summary": "Normal CD4+/CD56+ status and lack of skin/endocrine findings slightly reduce lymphoid neoplasm likelihood, as these markers could differentiate lymphoid from other blast-related conditions."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would a blast count of 35% in chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm (typically <20% blasts) argue against this diagnosis?", "summary": "A 35% blast count exceeds typical thresholds for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms in chronic/accelerated phases (usually <20%), providing moderate evidence against this diagnosis."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the normal serum calcium and BUN levels inconsistent with plasma cell disorder (which often shows hypercalcemia and renal dysfunction)?", "summary": "Normal calcium and BUN levels reduce the likelihood of plasma cell disorders, which frequently present with hypercalcemia and renal impairment."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.583], ["B3", 0.338], ["B1", 0.062]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "Patient is hemodynamically stable with no airway, respiratory, or shock compromise. No time-critical syndrome patterns (e.g., stroke, septic shock, tension pneumothorax) are present. Constitutional symptoms and lab findings suggest hematologic malignancy but do not require immediate life-saving intervention.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch posterior (0.583) < 0.75 and dangerous alternative B1 (posterior=0.062) remains unresolved. No info exhaustion (untried discriminators exist). Depth limit (3) not reached yet."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.7, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.5831736044398012, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Acute myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Chronic myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'hypercalcemia', 'renal dysfunction'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.583}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "posterior": 0.583, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Normal CD4+/CD56+ status and lack of skin/endocrine findings slightly reduce lymphoid neoplasm likelihood, as these mar…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=100%, source=primekg]\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase): bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  and night sweats → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  and night sweats → pain → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  and night sweats → fatigue → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts"}
